%erg v1
Title: Rename modalities so decomposed signals RAG and census signals naive prompt
Status: open
Created: 2026-04-11
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-04-11T10:20Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

Two naming choices in the experiment vocabulary mislead readers (and
agents working on the codebase):

1. **"Decomposed" hides that it's a RAG variant.** The decomposition
   strategy (`src/aedist/query_decomposed.py`) injects the same RAG
   corpus as `query_rag.py` into the system message — only the user
   prompt is split into 3 fuel sub-queries. From the name alone,
   "decomposed" sounds like a parametric trick. It is not. It is a
   RAG sub-strategy. Outputs live in `experiments/outputs/decomposed/`,
   the corresponding sweep is `decomposed`, the report section is
   "Décomposition par type de combustible" — none of these signal RAG.
   Consequence: when comparing it against `parametric` / `rag` /
   `web_search` regimes (ablation Phase 1), the reader has to know
   the implementation to place it in the right column.

2. **"Census" is a more naive prompt than "base", but the names
   suggest the opposite.** "Census" reads as the rigorous/exhaustive
   variant; "base" reads as the stripped-down baseline. In practice
   the relation is reversed — `prompts/prompt_structured.txt` (used
   by the census sweep) is the terse one-liner ("Provide a table
   listing all thermal power plants in Vietnam..."), while the
   "base" prompt in the ablation framework is the enriched starting
   point that prompt modules attach to. A reader scanning tables
   sees "census > base" and infers more rigour where there is less.

## Actions

1. Decide replacement names. Suggestions (not prescriptive):
   - `decomposed` → `rag_decomposed` or `rag_by_fuel` (signals it
     is a RAG variant, scoped by fuel category)
   - `census` (the prompt/sweep) → `naive` or `terse` or
     `unstructured_prompt`. Or rename `base` to `enriched_base`
     and leave `census` alone if "census" carries domain meaning
     elsewhere (the dataset name).
2. Audit the touch surface before renaming. Grep for the strings:
   ```
   experiments/experiments.toml
   experiments/outputs/{decomposed,census}/
   src/aedist/query_decomposed.py
   src/aedist/*.py (any sweep dispatching)
   report/report.tex (section headings, table captions, prose)
   measurements.jsonl (strategy field)
   tickets/*.erg (Blocked-by refs, body text)
   ```
3. Renames need a migration commit, not a sed-and-pray:
   - rename output directories with `git mv`
   - update `experiments.toml` set/sweep names
   - update `strategy` field values written by the harness
   - regenerate or backfill `measurements.jsonl` so historical rows
     match the new vocabulary (or document the pre/post split)
   - update report tex and regenerate PDF
4. Update `MEMORY.md` and any user-visible memories that name the
   old vocabulary.

## Test

After rename, a fresh reader should be able to look at the report
table for the three regimes and place each method in the correct
column without consulting the source.

## Exit criteria

- New names committed across code, config, outputs, and report.
- Report regenerated and visually checked.
- No stale references to old names in active tickets or memory.
- Decision recorded in commit message (which name was picked and
  why), so the rename does not get re-litigated in three months.
